{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"communist-metalline-cranefly","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nUnder sustained write load the enrich drain holds its advisory drain lock across NO-TIMEOUT LLM calls (observed 437s and 131s holds on the live store), making the system structurally single-flight: every per-write spawned drain child hits the held lock and exits `{\"skipped\": \"lock-held\"}`. Throughput ceiling is 5 tickets per multi-minute lock hold, below the enqueue rate (every certification enqueues; the stale-digest self-heal re-enqueues), so a ~1,046-entry standing backlog accumulated. The operator approved the \"option 2\" restructure designed and experimentally validated on bug 6148-5d81-8e80-41e8 (alias draughty-callous-tanager): the prototype cut the drain-lock hold 19.8s -> 1.5-2.9s (13x) and two concurrent drains on 10 tickets made exactly 10 LLM calls (zero redundant spend). The shape matches the Postgres SKIP LOCKED job-queue pattern (solid_queue/oban/pg-boss/good_job): reserve-short -> process-unlocked -> finalize-short + lease recovery.\n\n## Operator rulings (recorded before implementation)\n\n- OQ1 (concurrent-drain cap): small cap per the design's recommendation — 2-3 concurrent drainers, bounded by a small guard in the collect phase.\n- OQ2 (backlog): NO bulk backlog job — the ~1,046 standing backlog drains through normal workflow traffic; steady-state throughput must exceed the enqueue rate.\n- OQ3 (batch cap): raised from 5 but BOUNDED, sized to the lease window (batch ~ lease_duration / worst-case per-item LLM time, concretely ~20-25). Rationale: drains run as children of ordinary writes (unbounded = hours + ~1k LLM calls billed to one random write), lease expiry mid-run causes duplicate claims, crash blast radius.\n- OQ4: separate from the merged option-1 probe (change 2140) — `has_pending_enrichment` semantics are NOT touched.\n- OQ5 (unruled, judgment): stale-at-writeback re-enqueue uses soak 0 (immediate retry), matching the validated prototype and the existing self-heal path's posture.\n- OQ6 (unruled, judgment): no separate drain-call timeout knob — `cfg.timeout_s` (default 600s) already bounds each call, and with the lock no longer held across calls a hung provider blocks only its own batch. Adding a knob is config surface this change deliberately avoids.\n\n## Approach\n\nRestructure `drain()` in `src/rebar/llm/enrich_drain.py` into three phases (behavior-preserving extraction along the existing call-graph seams, which also serves the complexity ratchet):\n\n- COLLECT (under the drain advisory lock; seconds): concurrency guard (skip with `{\"skipped\": \"concurrency-cap\"}` when >= 3 distinct live-lease drainer_ids already exist), self-heal re-enqueues, `pending_enrichment` scan, per-ticket optimistic `claim()` up to the batch cap, and a `digest_sidecar.content_hash` snapshot of each claimed ticket's state. Release the lock.\n- ENRICH (NO lock held): run `enrich()` per claimed item; capture per-item result or exception.\n- FINALIZE (no drain lock; store write lock per append as today, unchanged): re-read the ticket; if its content hash changed since collect -> skip the emit and re-enqueue with soak 0 (counted as `stale_skipped` in the summary); else `ds.emit` (with the revalidated state) + `mark_done`. Exceptions keep the existing disposition (`_record_item_failure`: transient -> lease-expiry retry; permanent CHANGE_INPUT -> tombstone).\n\nBatch cap: raise `DEFAULT_OVERLAP_DRAIN_BATCH` 5 -> 20, and clamp the effective per-run batch to a lease-derived bound `max(1, lease_ttl_s // 40)` (= 22 at the default 15-min lease, inside the operator's ~20-25 window; 40s is the worst-case per-item budget for the trivial-class digest call) so a configured batch can never outrun its lease mid-run.\n\nUNCHANGED: store write lock semantics, queue event schema (ENQUEUE/CLAIM/DONE), `REBAR_LLM_OVERLAP_DRAIN` off|async|always semantics, the `REBAR_LLM_OVERLAP_DRAIN_BATCH` door's meaning (claim-window size), `has_pending_enrichment` (option-1, change 2140), the MCP surface, and the operation-linked trigger (portability holds: no CI-provider dependency; the drain remains spawned from ordinary store writes). Existing per-write spawned drain children become productive concurrent drainers (bounded by the OQ1 guard) with no new spawner or scheduler.\n\nThroughput argument (OQ2): pre-change ceiling is 5 items per lock-hold of minutes (single-flight). Post-change: up to 3 concurrent drainers x ~20 items per batch, with the lock held only for the ~1.5-2.9s collect window — items process in parallel with further collects. Enqueue sources (certifications + self-heal) produce well under that rate, so the standing backlog drains through normal traffic.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/enrich_drain.py` — `drain()` split into collect/enrich/finalize helpers + concurrency guard + lease-derived batch clamp.\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — `DEFAULT_OVERLAP_DRAIN_BATCH` 5 -> 20 (the env door keeps its name and meaning).\n- `tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py` — new mechanism pins + updates to the two tests that hardcode batch=5 (pin the mechanism via `REBAR_LLM_OVERLAP_DRAIN_BATCH=5` instead).\n- `docs/llm-framework.md` — a short note on the three-phase drain and the concurrency bound.\n\n## Testing\n\nAll deterministic (counted/instrumented, no wall-clock timing):\n\n- Lock-not-held-across-LLM: a runner whose `run()` attempts `_acquire_advisory_lock` on the same tracker — acquisition must SUCCEED during every LLM call (it fails today), proving the drain lock is released before the enrich phase.\n- Concurrent zero-duplicate: a re-entrant runner triggers a second `drain()` from inside the first drain's enrich phase (the exact concurrent-drainer shape); assert total LLM calls == ticket count and each ticket enriched exactly once (claim arbitration).\n- Stale-writeback revalidation: a runner edits the ticket during `run()`; assert the digest emit is skipped (`stale_skipped == 1`, no digest recorded), the entry is re-enqueued pending, and a follow-up drain enriches it fresh.\n- Lease-expiry recovery: a claim with an already-expired lease is re-picked and processed by the next drain.\n- Concurrency guard: with 3 distinct live-lease drainer claims planted, `drain()` returns `{\"skipped\": \"concurrency-cap\"}`; with 2 it proceeds.\n- Batch clamp: with `REBAR_LLM_OVERLAP_DRAIN_BATCH=1000` the effective batch reported by `drain()` equals the lease-derived bound (22 at default lease); default config yields batch 20.\n- Existing suite (failure disposition, self-heal, lock-held skip, fairness, worktree lock identity) stays green — the phases are behavior-preserving for every pre-existing contract.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The drain advisory lock is never held across an LLM call: pinned by an instrumented-runner test that acquires the drain lock from inside `run()` and asserts success for every call in a batch (checked: `pytest tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py`).\n- [ ] Two concurrent drains produce zero duplicate LLM calls: the re-entrant-runner claim-arbitration test counts exactly N calls for N tickets, each ticket enriched once (checked: pytest, counted not timed).\n- [ ] Stale-at-writeback revalidation: a ticket edited between claim and finalize gets NO stale digest emit; the entry is re-enqueued (soak 0) and the summary reports `stale_skipped` (checked: pytest).\n- [ ] Lease-expiry recovery: an entry claimed by a crashed drainer (expired lease) is processed by a subsequent drain (checked: pytest).\n- [ ] The effective batch cap is ~20-25 and lease-derived: default 20, and any configured value is clamped to `max(1, lease_ttl_s // 40)` (checked: pytest on the clamp + the config default).\n- [ ] Concurrent drainers are bounded: a 4th drainer skips with `{\"skipped\": \"concurrency-cap\"}` while <= 3 distinct live-lease drainers exist (checked: pytest).\n- [ ] Steady-state throughput exceeds the enqueue rate: argued quantitatively in a ticket comment from the measured collect-window hold (1.5-2.9s) x 3 drainers x batch 20 vs the enqueue sources; before/after lock-hold numbers recorded on the ticket (checked: evidence comment present before close).\n- [ ] Config doors, queue event schema, MCP surface, and `has_pending_enrichment` are unchanged: no new/renamed env keys or event types in the diff; option-1 probe untouched (checked: `git diff` review + existing config/queue tests green).\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the targeted pytest all pass; the module-size gate (<=800 LOC) and complexity ratchet (new=0, increased=0) hold (checked: CI Verified +1).","id":"a2fc-7ea9-9029-4647","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"enrich-drain: collect/enrich/finalize restructure — LLM calls outside the drain lock"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787460966236157001,"uuid":"0ff375ee-8fa0-4f74-ad4b-8602672d141b"}